 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: delivery maintains a socket session per connected browser and pushes each committed event revision as it lands. This keeps update latency low without a recurring realtime-vendor fee, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnection behavior.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Three consequences shape the sibling components. The change store keeps a replayable revision history so a browser that reconnects can fetch what it missed. Delivery owns socket sessions: admission, disconnection, and reliable publication of each committed revision to the sessions that should see it. Browser sync handles reconnect and catch-up, and keeps a visibly stale board while disconnected so a connection failure is never mistaken for an unchanged board. The 15-second freshness goal is a floor, not the design target.
 
